{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]],"date-time":"2026-08-21T14:50:04Z","timestamp":1787323804268,"version":"build-2736575974"},"reference-count":41,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"37","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,5]],"date-time":"2026-07-05T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1783209600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,5]],"date-time":"2026-07-05T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1783209600000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100003593","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient\u00edfico e Tecnol\u00f3gico","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["141040\/2022\u20100"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["141040\/2022\u20100"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100003593","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100003593","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient\u00edfico e Tecnol\u00f3gico","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["303177\/2025\u20100"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["303177\/2025\u20100"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100003593","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["J of Applied Polymer Sci"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Injection molding is widely used for polymer components, yet producing dimensionally accurate poly(lactic acid) (PLA) parts remains challenging because shrinkage and warpage arise from thermal contraction, crystallization\u2010induced densification, and relaxation of frozen\u2010in amorphous structure. Post\u2010molding annealing can increase crystallinity and heat resistance, but may also induce secondary shrinkage and embrittlement. Here, two commercial injection\u2010molding PLA grades with different crystallization behavior were processed under identical molding and packing conditions, while only the annealing protocol was varied between 60\u00b0C and 120\u00b0C. DSC showed different crystallization tendencies after thermal\u2010history erasure, with crystallinity of approximately 3% for PLA_a and 20% for PLA_c, although as\u2010molded specimens showed similar crystallinity of 14%\u201317%. Isothermal DSC confirmed faster crystallization of PLA_c, with half\u2010times 3\u20135 times shorter than PLA_a at 100\u00b0C and 120\u00b0C. Annealing increased crystallinity to approximately 68% for PLA_c and 50% for PLA_a after 3\u2009h at 120\u00b0C. However, PLA_a showed stronger secondary shrinkage, reaching 2%\u20132.3%, whereas PLA_c remained below 1%. Enthalpy\u2010recovery analysis, POM, SEM, MFI, TGA, and FTIR demonstrate that annealing produces a grade\u2010dependent trade\u2010off between crystallinity, dimensional accuracy, and mechanical performance. Impact strength peaks at 90\u00b0C, whereas tensile performance decreases at 120\u00b0C, confirming crystallinity alone cannot predict dimensional stability reliably.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/app.71150","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,6]],"date-time":"2026-07-06T06:58:58Z","timestamp":1783321138000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Thermal Annealing of Injection\u2010Molded\n                    <scp>PLA<\/scp>\n                    Grades: Trade\u2010Offs Between Crystallinity Development, Dimensional Stability, Secondary Shrinkage, and Mechanical Performance"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"143","author":[{"given":"Edson A.","family":"dos Santos Filho","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Polymer Processing Laboratory, Department of Materials Engineering Federal University of Campina Grande  Campina Grande Brazil"},{"na